WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.970
 

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:03.101
L RAFAEL REIF: Good morning.

00:00:03.101 --> 00:00:06.920
Look, it's a little cold and wet
out there, so let's warm

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:07.650
ourselves up.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.250
Good morning!

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.100
AUDIENCE: Good morning.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:10.890
L RAFAEL REIF: That's better.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:14.600
To the graduates of 2013,
congratulations.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.120
One more time you'll hear it,
and you deserve to hear it

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.030
several times today.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:23.550
I know I'm the last thing
standing between you and your

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:28.330
diplomas, so even though I have
a great deal I want to

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.100
tell you, I will be brief.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.950
To everyone who has come here
this morning to celebrate the

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.730
graduates of 2013,
welcome to MIT.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.840
And to the parents and families
of today's graduates,

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.420
congratulations to
you, as well.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.360
It's great.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.700
It's great to have you all here
in Killian Court for this

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.960
tremendously important
occasion.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.350
Now the last time that I was out
here with a big group of

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:07.290
people, we were having a
huge snowball fight.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.900
I know that many members
of the class of 2013

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.490
were part of it.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:19.750
To those of you who got
me wet, consider this

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:21.740
weather as round two.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.020
 

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.740
And I personally invite you to
come back next winter for a

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:30.990
healthy rematch.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.520
 

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.960
I'm told that before we let you
graduate, I'm expected to

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.350
give our graduating
class a charge.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.220
That sounds to me like one last
problem set, one last

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:52.140
PSET, MIT is giving
you, all of you.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.640
That is quite a fun challenge.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.960
I'm also told not to worry too
much about what I'm telling

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.210
you, as you're likely to forget
whatever I say two

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.210
seconds after I'm done.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.080
So that is yet another
fun challenge I'm

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.510
eager to take on.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:20.050
Let me start by welcoming you
again to the MIT family, even

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.890
though you joined our family the
moment you registered as

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:29.290
an MIT student for the
very first time.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.740
And this is no ordinary
family.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:37.080
First of all, the MIT
family is big.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:45.190
Its core members include MIT's
127,000 alumni world-wide,

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:48.860
including those over there
looking spiffy in their

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.240
colorful red jackets.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.540
 

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.280
Spiffy and wet, I might add.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.750
Our MIT family also includes the
remarkable members, past

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:07.130
and present, of the MIT faculty,
as well as our

00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:11.130
students, post-docs,
and staff.

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.790
Now, as you all know,
this is not a family

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:17.320
you are born into.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.570
You have to earn being a
member of our family.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:25.450
And our big family opens its
arms to all of those who are

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.310
committed and dedicated
to MIT's mission,

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.340
principles, and values.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.970
This family you chose to join
years ago has been busy.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:43.270
Over the years, members of the
MIT family have been awarded

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:48.780
the Nobel Prize 78 times.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:55.230
That is over 9% of all Nobel
Prizes ever awarded.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:59.800
There are only three countries
with more Nobel Prizes than

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.610
the MIT family--

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:07.600
the US, the UK, and Germany.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:13.540
Also at last count, living MIT
alumni have founded about

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:17.690
26,000 active companies.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:18.529
Do the math.

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:23.270
That's one company for every
five living graduates.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.050
Those companies provide jobs
for 3.3 million people, and

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.460
they produce $2 trillion
in global revenues.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.970
And that was before Dropbox.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.600
 

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.580
I don't have to tell you
that our big family

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.560
likes to do big things.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.680
This family is ambitious--

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:53.250
incredibly, beautifully,
disruptively, ambitious.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:58.190
There is simply no
place like MIT.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:03.210
Our great family embraces many
styles and personalities.

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:07.330
I have met many of you, the
graduates of 2013, in

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:09.520
the last few years.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.090
When I ask you, what
is it that you like

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.126
the most about MIT?

00:05:14.126 --> 00:05:17.930
you have answered, each other.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:22.680
You have also told me that you
did not know until you got to

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:29.920
MIT that smartness comes in
so many different ways.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:34.090
So you know MIT, and you know
each other very well.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.980
And you know that we all share
some deep values--

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:43.150
a commitment to excellence,
integrity, meritocracy, an

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:47.930
open spirit of collaboration,
a strong desire to make a

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:52.870
positive impact, and a sense of
responsibility to make the

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:56.560
world a better place.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.730
In fact, Mr. Sal Khan, one
of our very accomplished

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:04.550
graduates and last year's
commencement speaker, came

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:07.780
back here to visit
this spring.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:12.980
And he explained that he can
always spot someone from MIT,

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:18.500
even without checking for a
brass rat, because, he said,

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:23.770
when you tell them that some
problem is impossible, their

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.720
eyes light up.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.536
That is you.

00:06:28.536 --> 00:06:31.810
That is MIT.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:36.150
The MIT family is famous
for all these things--

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.790
world famous.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:43.970
As you go out into the world,
you may find that these family

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.780
characteristics set you apart.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:51.790
They will make you different,
and I believe they will serve

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.560
you very well.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.180
As you leave us today, however,
I want you to

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:02.600
appreciate another
side of MIT, too.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:09.270
It came into focus for me this
year like never before.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:13.510
I began to notice it last fall,
when a student named

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:17.440
Lydia K. published a blog
that captured the

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.900
attention of the campus.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:23.330
She described an intense
period of sadness and

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:24.680
self-doubt.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:31.740
And hundreds of MIT students
reached out to her in support.

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:37.960
I saw this side of MIT after
the Boston Marathon attack.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:43.270
I received letters from total
strangers who had fled the

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:47.830
bombing site, and stumbled
into our fraternities and

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:50.830
sororities in Boston.

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:53.920
And they felt compelled to write
because they were so

00:07:53.920 --> 00:08:00.250
moved by our students'
generosity and kindness.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:05.020
I also saw this other aspect
of MIT in the great wave of

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:09.470
support and sympathy across our
community for every one

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:14.560
affected by the Boston
Marathon attack.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:19.900
Then a few days later, the
tragedy of the marathon

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:23.710
bombing arrived at
our own campus.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:27.220
And the whole world
saw what I saw--

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:32.220
the extraordinary outpouring of
respect and gratitude for

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:36.179
our beloved MIT police.

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:39.919
And the loving sympathy that
flowed from the heart of this

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:44.770
family to the family
of Officer Collier.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:51.160
I've never felt so proud
to be part of MIT.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:54.570
Today, I can tell you for
certain that the world will

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:58.130
respect you for what you
know, and for what

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:01.220
you know how to do.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:07.120
But I also want the family of
MIT to be famous for how we

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:09.120
treat people--

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.590
famous for sympathy,
humility, decency,

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:17.010
respect, and kindness.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:22.160
I'm certain that no matter what
I say, you will take on

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.520
important problems.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:29.280
I'm certain that each of you
will, in your own way, honor

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.050
the great privilege we all
share in being here.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:37.780
And I'm certain you will use
what you have learned - in

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:41.830
your labs and classrooms, in
your living rooms, on the

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:45.410
playing fields, in your
activities, and in late night

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:49.090
conversations with friends - I'm
certain you will use what

00:09:49.090 --> 00:09:53.980
you've learned here to make
the world a better place.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:59.900
Yet, I want to charge you to
do something else, too.

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:03.960
And this is your last PSET.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:07.690
As you go out into society,
I want you to

00:10:07.690 --> 00:10:10.560
change the source code.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:14.240
I want you to rewire the
circuits, rearrange the

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:17.760
molecules, reformulate
the equation.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:21.930
In short, I want you to hack the
world until you make the

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.981
world a little more like MIT.

00:10:25.981 --> 00:10:32.659
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:32.659 --> 00:10:36.970
L RAFAEL REIF: More daring
and more passionate, more

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:42.150
rigorous, inventive and
ambitious, more humble, more

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:46.600
respectful, more generous,
and more kind.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:50.060
If Sal Khan were here, he would
advise me to tell you

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:53.970
that this is impossible.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:59.290
On this wonderful day, I'm proud
of all of you, as I know

00:10:59.290 --> 00:11:01.520
you are ready.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:07.610
So now, go out there, join the
world, find your calling,

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:11.150
solve the unsolvable,
shape the future,

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:12.900
take the high road.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:16.360
And you will continue to make
your family, including your

00:11:16.360 --> 00:11:19.860
MIT family, proud.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:25.400
To the graduating class of
2013, my first class,

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:26.900
congratulations.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:37.097
[APPLAUSE]

